From 0d16f97e61f5588751ec53111df2af16a601bacc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anees Iqbal <hello@aneesiqbal.ai>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 07:49:48 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 49/68] vulkan: a pipeline-cache blob past the bound is
 rebuilt, not parked
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

x86/Vulkan. The on-disk VkPipelineCache warm-start blob could climb to just
under its bound and stay there for the life of the machine, where every
pipeline creation costs several times what it costs at a right-sized blob.

The bound was used for both halves of the policy: the save refused to write
anything larger, and the load refused to read anything larger. Because the
save can never write past it, the file can never exceed it, so the load's
refusal could not fire and the "one cold boot rebuilds a small blob"
self-heal the comment described was unreachable. The blob asymptoted at the
cap and the device paid for it on every boot, on the drain thread, while it
holds the device lock.

Six interleaved driven macos-15 Maps boots, host quiesced, arms differing
only in the blob the boot starts from (misses = pipeline_misses +
compute_pipeline_misses):

  loaded    settled   misses   pl_compile_us   per compile
  14.4 MB   14.4 MB     285       0.601 s        2.11 ms
  14.4 MB   14.4 MB     285       0.603 s        2.12 ms
   0.00 MB   3.3 MB     278       0.158 s        0.57 ms
   0.00 MB   3.4 MB     301       0.153 s        0.51 ms
   3.3 MB    3.4 MB     275       0.156 s        0.57 ms
   3.3 MB    3.4 MB     283       0.148 s        0.52 ms

The arms are disjoint by 3.9x with miss counts within 2.5 % of each other,
so this is what a compile costs and not how many happen: 0.45 s of extra
drain CPU on every boot. 14.4 MB is under the old 16 MiB bound, which is why
nothing ever fired. Note also that a right-sized blob and no blob at all are
indistinguishable here (0.545 vs 0.540 ms) — the warm start pays on macos-13,
where the table already in this file measured 0.73 against 1.41 ms, and does
not pay on macos-15. It stays for that reason; what cannot stay is a size at
which it charges 3.9x.

So:

- the bound drops to 8 MiB, set from the two measured single-boot working
  sets (3.85 MB macos-13, 3.4 MB macos-15) rather than from headroom above
  them, and well under the smallest size at which a penalty has been measured;
- PIPELINE_CACHE_WORKING_SET_BYTES declares that working set separately, with
  a const assertion that the bound leaves room for two of them — a bound at or
  below one boot's working set would make every boot overflow and silently
  turn the warm start into dead code with a cost;
- overflow now discards the on-disk blob instead of only declining the write,
  so the next boot is cold and the one after it warms at this workload's own
  size. Discarding resets the newest-wins guard: it holds the largest length
  landed and supersedes anything smaller, so without the reset the rebuilt
  (smaller) blob would never land and the rail would be cold for the life of
  the process rather than for one boot.
- a failed removal reports vk_pipeline_cache_discard, separate from
  vk_pipeline_cache_too_large: the latter says the rebuild was decided, only
  the former says it did not happen.

Verified: cargo test -p reims-vgpu --no-default-features --features
backend-vulkan,host-window -- --test-threads=1, all 9 binaries ok (lib 2224;
the 3 ignored are the gitignored wire fixtures). Both new tests were shown to
fail with the guard reset stubbed out and pass with it. clippy -D warnings
exit 0 on all three arms (native vulkan, aarch64-apple-darwin backend-metal,
x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu). scripts/feature-matrix: all arms PASS.

Live-verified end to end on macos-15 (x86/Vulkan, maps-probe): the parked
15,133,599-byte blob was planted and the boot reported

  vk_pipeline_cache_load reason=vk_pipeline_cache_too_large \
      bytes=15133599 cap=8388608
  OFF vk_pipeline_cache_load bytes=0

so the load-side refusal now fires where it previously could not; the boot ran
cold, the saves rebuilt the file to 3,539,512 bytes, and that boot measured
misses=292 pl_compile_us=153742, i.e. 0.53 ms a compile against the parked
arm's 2.11 ms.

What the saving shows up as, since a frame counter is too blunt for it:
`slow_tranches`, the count of drain tranches past the 8333 us slow mark, was
86 and 76 on the parked arm against 63/59 cold and 59/58 right-sized. The
arms are disjoint — the parked minimum exceeds every other boot's maximum —
which is the expected shape for a cost paid on the drain thread while it
holds the device lock. At two boots against four that ordering has a ~7 %
chance of arising by luck, so it is corroboration of the mechanism rather
than an independent result. The stamp-publish latency bands did *not*
separate (lt16ms: 195/211 parked against 195/222 cold and 197/186
right-sized), so the stall is not reaching the guest's stamp waits.

Not verified: frame rate. `fresh` overlaps across all three arms at n=2
(parked 249/262, cold 251/234, right-sized 245/284), which is what the
elasticity note beside VBL_REPORT_EARLY predicts for a saving this size on a
workload where the device is ~33 % occupied — so this is a drain-CPU and
lock-hold result, not a demonstrated frame win. The macos-13 and Metal
pathways were not re-measured; the macos-13 numbers quoted are the ones
already recorded in this file.

